---
/**
 * foodcraft / GalleryBN — "Panday Coffee Works", the floor as a contact sheet.
 *
 * ROLE. The one section on the page that is nothing but the owner's own
 * photographs. It is FULL-BLEED: a ruled head on the spine (rail + one display
 * headline) and then, edge to edge with no side padding at all, a row of square
 * plates divided by hairlines — a contact sheet, not a mosaic. Nothing here is
 * rounded, nothing is gapped, and nothing floats: the tiles touch, and the
 * only thing between two photographs is a 1px rule.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="GALLERY" in
 * "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html":
 *
 *   section          border-bottom 1px #17181A, NO rail grid of its own
 *                                                            → .sec-full
 *   head             minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr)            → .sec
 *   rail             right hairline #C8C7C2, label bottom-aligned
 *                                                            → .rail / .rail-t
 *   head cell        padding 44px 34px 30px  (the 30px BOTTOM is this
 *                    section's own — .sec-body caps at 40)
 *   headline         Archivo 800, clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px), line-height 1,
 *                    letter-spacing -.03em, uppercase, margin 0, #17181A
 *   tile grid        repeat(6, minmax(0,1fr)), border-top 1px #C8C7C2
 *   tile             aspect-ratio 1/1, ground #DEDCD6, border-right 1px #C8C7C2
 *   caption          IBM Plex Mono 9px / .14em caps, padding 5px 9px,
 *                    on an opaque #17181A block, #EFEEEA           → .cap
 *
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE. Every colour the design names already
 * has a wrapper token holding the same value — #C8C7C2 is --line, #17181A is
 * --ink (and --rule-strong for the section rule), #DEDCD6 is --well, #EFEEEA is
 * --on-ink, #A8351B is --accent — and every one of them arrives on a shared
 * primitive (.sec-full, .rail-t, .plate, .cap, .h2). This file states GEOMETRY
 * only. No colour, not one, is chosen here.
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (every one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 *    group id 'gallery' — that group declares these four fields and nothing
 *    else) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   gallery.tag                        → the rust mono eyebrow (.kick)
 *   gallery.headline                   → the display h2, set:html
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.image   → data-image-field, one per plate
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.caption → the optional opaque ink caption block
 * That is the whole list. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook no
 * sidebar input owns, so no other path is written here.
 *
 * ── NOT SUPPLIED BY THIS COMPONENT ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * NOT ONE VISIBLE WORD, with the single stated exception of the rail index
 * below. The design's "The floor, documented" and its six slot placeholders are
 * the DESIGNER filling their own template; a real roastery gets a page that
 * says only what that roastery said. Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an
 * empty value removes the ELEMENT: no eyebrow over nothing, no empty caption
 * block on a photo, no head cell with nothing in it, and — see the rule note
 * below — no hairline under a head that is not there.
 *
 * ── DERIVED, not authored ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * THE RAIL INDEX "04 / Gallery" is the one string this file prints. It is
 * DECORATIVE and DERIVED, never an editable hook and never stored: the numeral
 * is this section's position in PageBN's render order (Hero 00, About 01,
 * Services 02, Why 03, Gallery 04 — Trust and Header carry no rail in the
 * design), and the word is the section's ROLE. The design's own flavour word
 * there is "Plates", which is a coffee-bar joke belonging to the demo; a rail
 * label cannot be edited, so it must not assert anything about a business —
 * "Gallery" is what this section IS on every site.
 *
 * ROW INDEXES. Rows with no image are dropped, and the ORIGINAL array index
 * travels with the survivor as `.i`. The editor writes the data-field path
 * verbatim into the draft, so a re-indexed path would patch a different row:
 * clearing row 2's photo would misdirect row 3's caption edits into row 2.
 * Nothing in this section is numbered on screen, so no display index is needed
 * — the rail index is the section's, not a row's.
 *
 * THE COUNT LADDER. The design hard-codes six slots; content does not.
 * derive-content-defaults hands over up to six photos and an owner may upload
 * one or eleven, so the design's six-up is the TOP of a ladder rather than the
 * only case. Below six the track count IS the photo count, so a short row can
 * never leave the sheet ragged; at six the grid is the design's, verbatim; above
 * six the extras flow into implicit rows and the LAST plate stretches over
 * whatever tracks its row left empty. The stretch is computed PER BREAKPOINT —
 * a six-track row, a three-track row and a two-track row run out at different
 * counts — and it is carried as three custom properties on that one figure
 * rather than as a dozen classes.
 *
 * ── THE SECTION GATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The section renders only when at least one row carries an IMAGE — the BODY
 * content, never the tag or the headline. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets
 * gallery.tag and gallery.headline on EVERY site, so a gate counting those
 * would ship a rail, an eyebrow and a display headline over an empty sheet.
 * Once photos exist the tag and headline render (still individually guarded) —
 * refusing them would strand two live sidebar inputs.
 *
 * ── DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE THINK WERE OVERSIGHTS ────────────────
 * · THE HAIRLINE BELONGS TO THE HEAD, not to the grid. The design draws it as
 *   `border-top` on the tile grid; here it is the head block's border-bottom,
 *   recoloured from --rule-strong to --line. With both present the rendering is
 *   identical (one --line hairline between head and photographs). With the head
 *   gone — no tag, no headline — the design's version would print a grey
 *   hairline immediately beneath the previous section's full-strength ink rule,
 *   a doubled edge under nothing. This way the sheet simply starts.
 * · THE HEAD BLOCK, RAIL INCLUDED, IS GUARDED as one unit. The rail is the
 *   page's spine and no section should drop it lightly — but this is a
 *   .sec-full section whose spine lives inside its head, and a rail is a ruled
 *   column with a label: rendering it beside an empty cell would be a rule and
 *   a label over nothing, which is exactly what the guard discipline forbids.
 *   In practice derive-content-defaults fills the tag on every site, so the
 *   head — and the spine — is there.
 * · THE EYEBROW IS NOT IN THE DESIGN ELEMENT, and that is not a liberty. The
 *   GALLERY head draws the h2 alone. But genericContentSchema declares
 *   `gallery.tag` as a live sidebar input and derive-content-defaults writes it
 *   on every site, so leaving it unrendered would wire a visible editor field to
 *   nothing. It is rendered on the shared .kick primitive — the design's own
 *   10px / .24em rust mono, the same mark the hero kicker uses — and it is
 *   guarded, so a site that clears it gets the design's bare headline back.
 * · THE 13px BETWEEN EYEBROW AND HEADLINE is applied only when the eyebrow is
 *   actually there (`.kick + .pcwg-h2`), so with no eyebrow the h2 keeps the
 *   design's `margin: 0` exactly. 13px is the hero's own kicker-to-headline
 *   step (.14em at its 92px display size) expressed at a fixed size, because
 *   an em here would scale with a headline that is half that size.
 * · THE HEADLINE KEEPS THE WRAPPER'S 20ch MEASURE. The GALLERY element states
 *   no max-width, but it does not need one: its demo string is 21 characters.
 *   An owner's headline is arbitrary, and this page has no container cap
 *   (--maxw is 100%), so releasing the measure would let one line of Archivo
 *   800 run the full width of a 1400px sheet — something the design never
 *   shows. The design's headline voice is explicitly STACKED (the hero breaks
 *   three lines, the about head breaks three), and the wrapper's 20ch is that
 *   voice as a rule. The measure is therefore inherited, not restated, and the
 *   wrapper's own ≤760 release of it still applies.
 * · NO GAP. The family signature elsewhere is gapped tiles; this design rules
 *   them instead — `border-right` on each plate and nothing between. A gap here
 *   would break the contact sheet into cards, which is the one thing this
 *   template is not. The right-most hairline lands on the page edge, as in the
 *   design.
 * · NO HOVER LIFT. PageBN ships no transition on .ph — unlike its siblings —
 *   and the design specifies no hover treatment for a plate. A photograph that
 *   leaps under the cursor is a soft-template gesture, and this template is a
 *   technical document. Omitted deliberately, not forgotten.
 * · THE 440px CEILING on a plate's height is the one number here that the
 *   design could not state, because the design only ever draws six-across. A
 *   1/1 plate takes its height from its track, so at one or two photos a square
 *   would print a two-metre block down the page. 440px is the design's own
 *   tallest fixed plate: the design holds exactly three image slots, and their
 *   grounds are 380px (hero, under a 56vh height), 440px (about) and this 1/1
 *   grid. The one larger fixed height in the file — the LOCATION panel's 460px
 *   — is the drawn map face, which holds no photograph. So no gallery plate can
 *   ever exceed a plate the design itself draws. At the design's six-up the
 *   ceiling is inert until the viewport passes ~2640px, where a track is 440px
 *   wide (2640 / 6); below that width nothing is clipped and the tiles are
 *   exactly the squares the design specifies.
 * · min-height is zeroed on the plates. The wrapper floors .plate at 180px,
 *   which is EXACTLY a two-up track on a 360px screen (360 / 2, borders
 *   included) and taller than a track on anything narrower: a 320px phone gives
 *   160px tracks, where the floor would print a 160 x 180 upright rectangle in
 *   place of the square the design draws. The floor is released rather than
 *   lowered because every narrower device would otherwise need its own number.
 * · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER. This section renders no link, no button
 *   and no numeral, and the 'gallery' schema group declares no href field at
 *   all — so PAGE_ANCHORS / ANCHOR_ALIAS would have nothing to remap and would
 *   ship as dead frontmatter. Omitted rather than carried unused. The section's
 *   own id="gallery" IS one of the page's anchors, and is the target the header
 *   and footer link AT.
 * · THE CAPTION RESTATES TWO NUMBERS. .cap already carries this design's
 *   caption exactly — opaque ink block, mono, 9px, bottom-left, breaking a long
 *   string — but the GALLERY element sets .14em of tracking and 5px 9px of
 *   padding where the shared primitive sets .2em and 7px 12px. Those two values
 *   are restated and nothing else is; colour, position and family stay with the
 *   primitive.
 * · An apostrophe in an upload path would close the `url('…')` and blank the
 *   plate, so the path is percent-escaped FOR CSS ONLY. The raw value still
 *   travels into data-image-field, which is what the editor reads back.
 *
 * ── RESPONSIVE, and the clamp ladder traced ─────────────────────────────────
 * The wrapper owns the shared 760 and 520 pass (the rail goes horizontal, .h2
 * releases its measure). This section owns its own grid: SIX tracks collapse to
 * THREE at 900 and to TWO at 520. It stays two-up rather than one-up on a phone
 * because a contact sheet of squares is what this section is; a single 360px
 * column would turn six photographs into six screens of scrolling.
 *   headline  base clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px): 50px at ≥1389px, 32.4px at 900px,
 *             and it reaches its 28px floor at 778px, holding 28px from there
 *             down. The ≤520 rule is clamp(23px,6.4vw,28px): at exactly 520px
 *             it is capped to 28px — the same value the base holds there, so
 *             nothing jumps across the boundary — and from ~437px down it
 *             falls to a 23px floor. The ladder only ever descends.
 *   plate     440px ceiling, stated once and never restated, so no breakpoint
 *             can raise it. Track counts only ever decrease as the viewport
 *             narrows, so a plate's width never grows on a smaller screen.
 * At 360px nothing may push sideways: every track is minmax(0,…), the eyebrow
 * and the headline both break anywhere (stated at EVERY width, because the
 * wrapper's `body { overflow-x: hidden }` would CLIP an overrun rather than let
 * it scroll), and the caption's break rule arrives with .cap. This section
 * renders no button, so the button-label rule has nothing to apply to.
 *
 * ── ACCESSIBILITY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A plate is announced only when the owner gave it words. With a caption the
 * photo layer is role="img" named by THAT CAPTION — the owner's own words — and
 * the caption is also the visible block. With no caption the layer is HIDDEN
 * from the tree rather than announced as "image"; inventing "Roastery photo"
 * would publish a description of something nobody described. The two attributes
 * are mutually exclusive and BOTH conditional, so no literal aria-hidden ever
 * sits beside a conditional one.
 * The rail is aria-hidden as a whole: its rotated index is decoration derived
 * from render order, and the heading beneath it is the real announcement.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


const gallery = (content && content.gallery) || {};
const tag = typeof gallery.tag === 'string' ? gallery.tag.trim() : '';
const headline = typeof gallery.headline === 'string' ? gallery.headline.trim() : '';

type Tile = { image?: string; caption?: string };
// The legacy bare-array form lives at content.gallery ITSELF — not the
// `gallery` alias above, which is the object form's home. Both shapes reach
// production, so both are read.
const raw: Tile[] = Array.isArray(gallery.items)
  ? gallery.items
  : (Array.isArray(content?.gallery) ? (content.gallery as Tile[]) : []);

const tiles = raw
  .map((t, i) => ({
    image: (t && typeof t.image === 'string' ? t.image : '').trim(),
    caption: (t && typeof t.caption === 'string' ? t.caption : '').trim(),
    i,
  }))
  .filter((t) => Boolean(t.image));

const n = tiles.length;

// CSS-only escape; the raw value still goes into data-image-field.
const cssUrl = (s: string) => s.replace(/'/g, '%27');

/* Row completion, computed once per breakpoint (see THE COUNT LADDER above).
   Below the track count the grid narrows to the photo count, so the remainder
   is zero and nothing stretches; at or above it, the trailing plate takes its
   own track plus every track its row left empty. */
const rowFill = (cols: number) => {
  const c = Math.min(n, cols);
  if (c < 1) return 1;
  const rem = n % c;
  return rem === 0 ? 1 : c - rem + 1;
};
const spanD = rowFill(6);
const spanM = rowFill(3);
const spanS = rowFill(2);
const stretches = spanD > 1 || spanM > 1 || spanS > 1;
const spanVars = `--sd:${spanD};--sm:${spanM};--ss:${spanS};`;
---
{n > 0 && (
  <section class="sec-full pcwg" id="gallery">
    {(tag || headline) && (
      <div class="sec pcwg-head">
        <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rail-t">Gallery</span></div>
        <div class="sec-body pcwg-head-body reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick pcwg-kick" data-field="gallery.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 pcwg-h2" data-field="gallery.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      </div>
    )}

    <div
      class:list={['pcwg-grid', 'reveal', n < 6 && `pcwg-x${n}`]}
      data-delay="1"
    >
      {tiles.map((t, pos) => {
        const last = pos === n - 1 && stretches;
        return (
          <figure class:list={['plate', 'pcwg-frame', last && 'pcwg-last']} style={last ? spanVars : undefined}>
            <div
              class="ph"
              role={t.caption ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={t.caption || undefined}
              aria-hidden={t.caption ? undefined : 'true'}
              data-image-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.image`}
              style={`background-image:url('${cssUrl(t.image)}');`}
            ></div>
            {t.caption && (
              <figcaption class="cap pcwg-cap" data-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.caption`}>{t.caption}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* .pcwg carries NO declaration on purpose. .sec-full already states the
     design's full-bleed section and its 1px ink rule beneath, and this section
     has no side padding at all — the sheet runs edge to edge. The class stays
     as this section's root hook. */

  /* THE HEAD sits on the shared spine primitive (.sec + .rail + .sec-body), so
     the rail width, the rotated label and the collapse below 760 are the
     wrapper's, not this file's. Only two things are this section's own:
     the rule beneath the head is the design's GREY hairline rather than the
     spine's full-strength ink (the design draws it as the tile grid's
     border-top — see the frontmatter for why it lives here instead), and the
     head cell closes at 30px rather than .sec-body's 40px. */
  .pcwg-head { border-bottom-color: var(--line); }
  .pcwg-head-body { padding-bottom: clamp(20px, 2.6vw, 30px); }

  /* Owner-typed, 10px caps on wide tracking: a single unbroken word (a pasted
     URL) is far wider than its character count suggests, and the wrapper's
     `body { overflow-x: hidden }` would CLIP the tail rather than scroll to it.
     Stated at EVERY width. Only the wrapping is touched — restating .kick's
     size would cancel the wrapper's 520 pass, and restating its colour or
     tracking would drift the one mark this family is recognised by. */
  .pcwg-kick {
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The design's headline verbatim: its own smaller clamp, line-height 1 and
     margin 0. Weight, family, tracking, colour and the uppercase all arrive
     with .h2, and so does the 20ch measure — deliberately inherited, see the
     frontmatter. The step below the eyebrow applies ONLY when an eyebrow is
     there, so a bare head keeps the design's `margin: 0` exactly. */
  .pcwg-h2 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .pcwg-kick + .pcwg-h2 { margin-top: 13px; }

  /* THE CONTACT SHEET. The design's six equal tracks, ruled rather than gapped.
     minmax(0,…) on every track, never a bare fr: a background-image plate has
     no intrinsic width, but a caption does, and one long caption in a bare 1fr
     track would push the whole page sideways on a phone. */
  .pcwg-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(6, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  /* THE COUNT LADDER. Below six the tracks ARE the photos, so the sheet can
     never end on a hole. Each tier is a compound so it out-specifies the base
     above and survives into the breakpoints where it is already narrow enough. */
  .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x5 { grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  /* The plate. Ground, clip and the square (zero) corner all arrive with
     .plate; the design's 1/1 and its dividing hairline are stated here. The
     wrapper's 180px floor is released because it would break the square on a
     narrow two-up row, and the ceiling is the design's own tallest plate — see
     the frontmatter before changing either. */
  .pcwg-frame {
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    max-height: 440px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  /* The trailing plate closes a short last row. The three spans are carried as
     custom properties on that one figure so the breakpoints can each pick their
     own without a class per count; the `, 1` fallback means a property that
     never arrives computes to `span 1`, a single track — the same thing every
     other plate does. */
  .pcwg-grid .pcwg-last { grid-column: span var(--sd, 1); }

  /* The caption's two section-specific numbers. Everything else — the opaque
     ink block, the mono face, 9px, the bottom-left anchor, the break rule —
     is .cap's. Written as a DESCENDANT of the plate, not as the bare class:
     the wrapper states these on `html[data-page="BN"] :where(.plate) .cap`,
     which scores one attribute plus one class plus a type selector. A lone
     `.pcwg-cap` — one class plus the scope attribute Astro appends — matches
     that on the class/attribute count and then LOSES on the type selector, at
     every source order. The descendant form clears it outright. */
  .pcwg-frame .pcwg-cap {
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    padding: 5px 9px;
  }

  /* 900px · six tracks would print 150px thumbnails, so the sheet halves to
     three. The one- and two-photo tiers are NOT restated: they already
     out-specify this rule, so a lone photograph keeps the full width instead of
     shrinking into a third of a row beside two empty tracks. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .pcwg-grid,
    .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x3,
    .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x4,
    .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x5 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .pcwg-grid .pcwg-last { grid-column: span var(--sm, 1); }
  }

  /* 520px · two tracks. Still a sheet, not a stack: squares two-up are 180px
     on a 360px screen, which reads as a contact sheet, where one column would
     turn six photographs into six screens of scrolling. The headline takes its
     own floor here because this file's clamp out-specifies the wrapper's
     shared 760 pass, which is where the wrapper re-sizes .h2 (its 520 pass
     touches only .kick and .btn); it meets the base value exactly at 520px and
     only descends from there. Nothing may push sideways at this width: the tracks
     are minmax(0,…), the plates cannot exceed them, and both the eyebrow and
     the headline break anywhere at every width above. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .pcwg-grid,
    .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x3,
    .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x4,
    .pcwg-grid.pcwg-x5 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .pcwg-grid .pcwg-last { grid-column: span var(--ss, 1); }
    .pcwg-h2 { font-size: clamp(23px, 6.4vw, 28px); }
  }
</style>
